WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.110
Since 2008, the Marvel Cinematic Universe
has cemented its place as a pop culture juggernaut,

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:08.930
but while some of those movies are great,
a few of them are just a good excuse to get

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:11.630
some more popcorn during your 40-hour movie
marathon.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:16.490
Here’s every entry in the MCU, from the
superheroes to skip, down to the best must-see

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:18.289
movies of the bunch.

00:00:18.289 --> 00:00:20.220
Thor: The Dark World

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:25.050
In theory, 2013's The Dark World has everything
you could want - except fun.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.000
Chris Hemsworth is as charming as ever, and
it’s based on one of the greatest comic

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.470
book runs of all time.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:35.740
It’s got a Star Wars-style attack on Asgard
by spaceships flown by dark elves!

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:41.489
The rock monsters from Thor's very first comics
appearance! Thor and Loki teaming up for revenge

00:00:41.489 --> 00:00:45.800
against the villain that killed their mom,
climaxing in a fistfight in which Thor and

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.630
Malekith are literally punching each other
so hard that they land in other dimensions!

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:55.940
Unfortunately, while all of that sounds good
on paper, it's actually both super boring,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.520
and confusing to boot.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.739
Traits it shares with...

00:00:59.739 --> 00:01:01.489
The Incredible Hulk

00:01:01.489 --> 00:01:05.880
When Nick Fury showed up at the end of Iron
Man and hinted at the idea of a full-on shared

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.430
universe of superhero movies, fans were thrilled.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:13.210
And then The Incredible Hulk hit theaters
a month later and proved you should really

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:15.320
be careful what you wish for.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.180
It's not that the film is terrible.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.020
But it's something almost worse: forgettable.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.940
Which is a real shame when you consider that
it does so much right.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:27.270
For one thing, if you're going to make a movie
about a nerdy scientist with anger issues

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:31.979
so powerful they could level a whole town,
getting the guy who starred in Fight Club

00:01:31.979 --> 00:01:34.810
to play Bruce Banner is a pretty solid move.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:39.570
For another, the filmmakers realized audiences
didn't need a full-blown origin story to get

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:40.720
up to speed.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:45.220
Tying the Hulk’s origin into Captain America
and the Super Soldier program was the first

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:48.710
stumbling step towards building the full-on
shared universe.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:51.470
In the end, though, it just didn't quite land.

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:53.170
Iron Man 2

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:58.200
Sam Rockwell literally dancing across the
stage to present his army of Iron Man drones

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.880
is endlessly delightful, but there's a lot
in this movie that doesn't involve Justin

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.750
Hammer busting a move, too, and that's where
it fails to measure up to its predecessor.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:12.980
Hammer and Whiplash both serve as evil versions
of Tony Stark, bringing unnecessary complications

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.370
to the story.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:18.920
Even worse, the scene when Stark goes down
into his basement with a particle accelerator

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:24.160
to somehow build a new element might be the
goofiest thing in the entire MCU, and those

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.330
movies have a talking raccoon from space.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:32.230
At the same time, Iron Man 2 was a confident
stride towards the full MCU, introducing the

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:37.010
Black Widow and War Machine, and even teasing
Thor's arrival in the next film.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:41.099
There's a lot there to like, and even if it's
outweighed by the bad stuff, we'll always

00:02:41.099 --> 00:02:44.650
have Justin Hammer's dance moves.

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:45.889
Thor

00:02:45.889 --> 00:02:50.540
Iron Man 2 might've been the first big step
towards fleshing out the Marvel Universe,

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:54.430
but Thor was the first time we actually got
to see its scope in action.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:59.900
With Thor we got Asgard in all its glory,
complete with a rainbow laser bridge, epic

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:04.980
battles against frost giants, and—perhaps
most importantly—those big ol' Jack Kirby

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.709
hats that Norse gods apparently love to wear.

00:03:07.709 --> 00:03:11.159
It even gave us a viking god robot with a
face made of death lasers.

00:03:11.159 --> 00:03:17.140
And it introduced us to arguably the best
villain in the whole MCU, Loki.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:21.629
Like Thor: The Dark World, though, the original
Thor film somehow ended up feeling like less

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:22.879
than the sum of its parts.

00:03:22.879 --> 00:03:27.199
It should have been great, but it ended up
merely... okay.

00:03:27.199 --> 00:03:28.760
Iron Man 3

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:32.829
Iron Man 3 tried to do something different,
and it succeeded brilliantly.

00:03:32.829 --> 00:03:38.010
Unfortunately, what it was trying to do was
defy expectations by completely undermining

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:41.169
everything fans loved about Iron Man and the
MCU.

00:03:41.169 --> 00:03:45.900
Iron Man 3 explores how the smartest guy in
the room deals with living in a world that's

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.829
becoming something he can't predict.

00:03:47.829 --> 00:03:52.689
It's a movie about a superhero with an anxiety
disorder, which results in Iron Man spending

00:03:52.689 --> 00:03:57.900
the entire movie not wanting to be Iron Man,
and thus being Iron Man as little as possible.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:02.260
Plus, there was the whole fake Mandarin thing,
which some people loved... but most fans were

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.939
either confused or disappointed by.

00:04:04.939 --> 00:04:09.369
Director Shane Black deserves credit for his
bold vision, which made for a good movie - just

00:04:09.369 --> 00:04:11.629
not a good Marvel movie.

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:13.579
Doctor Strange

00:04:13.579 --> 00:04:18.030
When 2016's Doctor Strange was announced,
the big question for comics fans was how the

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:22.720
mind-bending psychedelic sorcerer of the mystical
Marvel Universe was going to fit into the

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:23.720
MCU.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:28.820
As comic fans know, things get pretty weird.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:35.259
And from a visual standpoint, the filmmakers
absolutely nailed it.

00:04:35.259 --> 00:04:39.710
The splitting realities in the movie's fight
scenes were beautifully weird, and the ghostly

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:43.800
ethereal plane where life-and-death battles
for the fate of the Earth could rage unseen

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.970
was great for showing how different Stephen
Strange's world was.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:52.139
From a story standpoint, though, it seemed
a little too familiar, as the story beats

00:04:52.139 --> 00:04:54.280
were basically Iron Man with magic.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:58.930
Still, you can do worse than copying one of
the most popular superhero films ever, and

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:04.460
the fantastic climax, with Strange's time-bending
bargain with Dormammu, is one of the best

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:07.189
finales in the MCU to date.

00:05:07.189 --> 00:05:09.120
Avengers: Age of Ultron

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.900
Juggling a massive cast of superheroes is
a difficult task, and while Avengers: Age

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:18.349
of Ultron doesn't manage it quite as well
as the other two Avengers films - or Captain

00:05:18.349 --> 00:05:22.939
America: Civil War - it does a pretty good
job of balancing character bits with all-out

00:05:22.939 --> 00:05:23.939
action.

00:05:23.939 --> 00:05:28.539
Sure, some parts seem half baked, like Tony
Stark apparently forgetting every lesson he

00:05:28.539 --> 00:05:32.349
learned in Iron Man 3, and Thor's weird spirit
quest.

00:05:32.349 --> 00:05:34.389
But it boasts a great final battle…

00:05:34.389 --> 00:05:36.900
"Is that the best you can do!?"

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:41.259
...not to mention the introduction of fan
favorite characters Vision and Scarlet Witch.

00:05:41.259 --> 00:05:46.259
Avengers: Age of Ultron isn't perfect, but
it still packs in a lot of fun.

00:05:46.259 --> 00:05:48.659
Guardians of the Galaxy vol. 2

00:05:48.659 --> 00:05:53.099
The first Guardians of the Galaxy movie proved
that the MCU could get weird and cosmic and

00:05:53.099 --> 00:05:55.220
still be incredibly entertaining.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:57.409
The second one proved that wasn't a fluke.

00:05:57.409 --> 00:05:59.580
If anything, it went even bigger.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:04.419
The first movie brought us stuff like the
Nova Corps and Ronan the Accuser, but Vol.

00:06:04.419 --> 00:06:08.860
2 has about a half hour of superheroes battling
Ego the Living Planet.

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:13.689
It's got waves of space drones swarming into
starship battles, a warp drive sequence that

00:06:13.689 --> 00:06:19.240
knows exactly how silly it is, and it features
the best Stan Lee cameo in cinema history

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:23.849
and the single best use of a Zune in anything,
ever.

00:06:23.849 --> 00:06:25.129
Ant-Man

00:06:25.129 --> 00:06:28.990
It's tempting to say Ant-Man shouldn't have
worked as well as it did, and that a second-tier

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:34.319
superhero with the ability to get really tiny
and talk to ants was a big surprise when he

00:06:34.319 --> 00:06:36.110
started to rake in billions.

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:39.180
Really, though, it's not that unexpected.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.759
After all, Marvel's first big movie success
came from a pretty obscure D-Lister from the

00:06:43.759 --> 00:06:46.389
pages of Tomb of Dracula.

00:06:46.389 --> 00:06:50.190
Audiences have always been interested in stories
that twist the expected superhero plot points

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:54.409
around into something new, and that's where
Ant-Man really delivers.

00:06:54.409 --> 00:06:59.160
As easy as it would've been to portray Scott
Lang as a microscopic version of Iron Man,

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:00.349
his story felt different.

00:07:00.349 --> 00:07:02.150
"Hey, are we the good guys?"

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:03.150
"Yeah."

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:03.740
"We're the good guys, right?"

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:05.080
"Yeah, we're the good guys."

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:07.220
"Feels kinda weird, you know?"

00:07:07.229 --> 00:07:12.060
The movie has its missteps, like going out
of its way to justify not having the Wasp

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:17.009
show up until the sequel, but on the other
hand, this is a movie in which the hero loses

00:07:17.009 --> 00:07:23.889
a fight with a toy train, and Chekhov's Gun
is actually Chekhov's 60-Ton Tank.

00:07:23.889 --> 00:07:25.389
The Avengers

00:07:25.389 --> 00:07:30.430
That The Avengers works at all is pretty impressive,
but that it works as well as it did?

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:32.029
That's basically a miracle.

00:07:32.029 --> 00:07:35.340
Even less than a decade later, it's difficult
to remember that this was the first time that

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:39.789
anything like this had been attempted: a movie
that combined characters who had been established

00:07:39.789 --> 00:07:43.539
in their own films, each with their own tone
and style.

00:07:43.539 --> 00:07:48.510
In bringing them together, Joss Whedon had
to balance the fantasy of Thor, the snarky

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:54.819
sci-fi of Iron Man, and the sincere superheroics
of Captain America, combining them all—along

00:07:54.819 --> 00:07:59.259
with Hulk, Hawkeye, and Black Widow—with
stakes that were high enough to bring everyone

00:07:59.259 --> 00:08:00.960
together for a single adventure.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:02.889
And this movie does it.

00:08:02.889 --> 00:08:06.770
Those big stakes mostly result from an army
of faceless aliens, but even that paid off

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:09.039
years later in Avengers: Infinity War.

00:08:09.039 --> 00:08:13.749
And, Loki getting Hulk-Smashed like a Looney
Tunes character was the most fun superhero

00:08:13.749 --> 00:08:15.120
movies had ever been.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:17.200
"Puny god."

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:22.870
Iron Man

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:25.660
It's safe to say Iron Man surprised us all.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:27.639
It's not that we expected it to be bad.

00:08:27.639 --> 00:08:31.690
Tony Stark's origin story is a pretty good
fit for a Hollywood action movie, it had a

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:37.010
great cast, and while director Jon Favreau
might've been best known for Elf, that wasn't

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:38.370
really a bad thing.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:43.050
But still, none of us expected it to be so
good that it would become the foundation of

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:44.750
the entire MCU.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:48.529
To say that it was an exceptionally powerful
launch would be an understatement.

00:08:48.529 --> 00:08:49.680
"...one."

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:51.200
[crash and grunting]

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.790
There are times when this feels less like
a "superhero" movie and more like a popcorn

00:08:56.790 --> 00:09:00.930
revenge flick where the hero just happens
to have a flying suit of armor, but it did

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:04.550
its job so well that those shortcomings are
easy to forgive.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:06.810
Captain America: The First Avenger

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:11.050
The First Avenger does origin stories better
than most, and a lot of that has to do with

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:12.220
Chris Evans.

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:17.410
In a franchise that's full of amazing casting
choices, Evans pulls off the incredible feat

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:22.500
of embodying a square-jawed, relentlessly
earnest product of the military-industrial

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:26.240
complex in a way that makes him impossible
not to like.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:30.589
For all the super-strength, that's a character
whose real super-power is making you believe

00:09:30.589 --> 00:09:33.290
in him, and that’s exactly what this movie
does.

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:36.760
“I can do this all day.”

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:41.010
As good as it was on its own, it also serves
as the proof-of-concept for the superhero

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:45.940
movie as a period piece, and gave the MCU
a history that went further back than 2008

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:47.870
and Tony Stark’s cave.

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:49.820
Guardians of the Galaxy

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:54.209
The first Guardians of the Galaxy movie manages
to balance so much stuff going on that you

00:09:54.209 --> 00:09:57.990
almost don't notice how complicated it is
for all the fun you're having.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:02.670
It’s got a team of lovable misfits dealing
with their own issues while fighting against

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:08.060
a massive threat, expansive interstellar empires,
and a retro feel supported by a classic rock

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:11.850
soundtrack that doesn't feel like it's wallowing
in the past.

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:16.430
All that, and still tells a story so fun that
it made Rocket Raccoon a household name.

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:20.860
It breaks the formula in every way it can,
to the point where it's a superhero team movie

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:25.010
that ends with a dance-off and the heroes
literally saving the universe with the power

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:26.560
of friendship.

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:29.070
Captain America: The Winter Soldier

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:33.450
Everything about The Winter Soldier reinforces
the idea that Captain America is exactly the

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:36.209
guy you want to have the power to save the
world.

00:10:36.209 --> 00:10:40.009
The notebook full of pop culture references
that he needs to look up to understand the

00:10:40.009 --> 00:10:41.180
world around him.

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:46.149
The friendship with Sam Wilson that starts
with bonding over their wartime experience.

00:10:46.149 --> 00:10:50.620
The loyalty to his friends that leads him
to risk his own life to save Bucky.

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:55.399
He even gives his opponents one last chance
to back out before he beats up an elevator

00:10:55.399 --> 00:10:56.980
full of traitors.

00:10:56.980 --> 00:11:01.550
All of these pieces did add up to a whole
that's incredible, even before you get to

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:05.000
how much of the movie is based around Steve
Rogers just kicking the living hell out of

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:06.259
bad guys.

00:11:06.259 --> 00:11:08.339
That stuff's pretty fun, too.

00:11:08.339 --> 00:11:10.290
Captain America: Civil War

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:14.910
Even though Steve Rogers and Bucky Barnes
are unquestionably the focus of Civil War,

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:17.899
there's a pretty convincing argument to be
made that this movie should've been called

00:11:17.899 --> 00:11:20.259
Avengers: Civil War instead.

00:11:20.259 --> 00:11:23.410
It has everyone in it, and they aren't just
cameos, either.

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:28.959
Ant-Man, Hawkeye, Scarlet Witch and War Machine
all get huge, character-developing moments

00:11:28.959 --> 00:11:33.579
in the middle of a superheroic Royal Rumble,
while the Avengers are split by a villain

00:11:33.579 --> 00:11:36.389
who wound up getting exactly what he wanted.

00:11:36.389 --> 00:11:40.639
And if that wasn't enough, it's also the movie
that introduced movie audiences to Black Panther

00:11:40.639 --> 00:11:41.660
and Spider-Man.

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:45.899
The characters are never flattened out to
fit, and the three-way fight at the end has

00:11:45.899 --> 00:11:51.910
not only some of the best action in the MCU,
but some of the most emotional moments, too.

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:53.300
Black Panther

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:58.300
After a his debut in Civil War, T’Challa’s
first solo movie came with some pretty high

00:11:58.300 --> 00:11:59.380
expectations.

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:04.649
Not only did it live up to them, it blew right
past them for one of the MCU’s best movies.

00:12:04.649 --> 00:12:08.850
Maybe it's because Chadwick Boseman's T'Challa
is a character struggling with the weight

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:10.810
of expectations himself.

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:15.069
The Black Panther is more than up for the
challenge, but the journey he takes to get

00:12:15.069 --> 00:12:19.520
there is full of compelling complications,
from the reveal that his father wasn’t the

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:24.410
flawlessly honorable man that he thought,
to a an enemy with some very justifiable anger

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:25.579
at the world around him.

00:12:25.579 --> 00:12:30.810
It’s a superhero struggle that goes far
beyond the punch-out battle in the climax.

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:35.670
Black Panther never shies away from its high-tech
comic book roots, but tells a story that's

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:39.230
very much rooted in the real world, and it
does it beautifully.

00:12:39.230 --> 00:12:41.550
Avengers: Infinity War

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:46.750
More than any other superhero movie, Infinity
War captures the feeling of an epic comic

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:48.230
book crossover.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:53.020
There are multiple interconnected stories
going on at the same time, each with its own

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:57.130
flavor, bridging different story arcs and
making sure every single character gets a

00:12:57.130 --> 00:12:58.740
moment in the spotlight.

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:02.470
When it comes together at the end, it’s
a fight across multiple planets where the

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:07.420
unbelievably high stakes become very real
for both the characters and the fans who have

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:09.910
been invested in these movies for a full ten
years.

00:13:09.910 --> 00:13:14.019
That’s a tough enough bit of storytelling
to pull off well even in comic books, and

00:13:14.019 --> 00:13:15.920
they’ve had decades of practice.

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:20.699
Seeing it done here, in a single movie that
still has that feeling of encompassing a whole

00:13:20.699 --> 00:13:21.829
universe?

00:13:21.829 --> 00:13:26.019
No other movie has ever done that, and it’s
hard to imagine another one doing it this

00:13:26.019 --> 00:13:27.019
well.

00:13:27.019 --> 00:13:28.910
Thor: Ragnarok

00:13:28.910 --> 00:13:33.850
To say that Ragnarok lifts heavily from Walt
Simonson's mid-'80s epic run on The Mighty

00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:35.149
Thor is putting it mildly.

00:13:35.149 --> 00:13:40.230
The only things in this movie that weren't
directly inspired by Simonson are the pieces

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:45.389
it pulls directly from Marvel's more recent
Planet Hulk storyline, right down to the Hulk's

00:13:45.389 --> 00:13:46.460
gladiator gear.

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:52.220
The thing is, Taika Waititi's film chops up
all those pieces and rearranges them into

00:13:52.220 --> 00:13:55.160
something that’s both different and genuinely
hilarious.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:57.790
"That's exactly what Doug used to say.

00:13:57.790 --> 00:13:59.529
See you later, new Doug."

00:13:59.529 --> 00:14:04.470
That part isn't surprising, considering that
before Ragnarok, Waititi was probably best

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:09.980
known for co-directing the vampire mockumentary
What We Do in the Shadows, but it takes advantage

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:13.940
of Chris Hemsworth's infuriatingly good comedic
timing.

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:17.560
"Ow."

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:19.459
"Just had to be sure."

00:14:19.459 --> 00:14:21.459
Spider-Man: Homecoming

00:14:21.459 --> 00:14:27.350
As good as it is on its own, the fact that
Peter Parker swings into action after 15 other

00:14:27.350 --> 00:14:30.950
movies means we don't have to waste time with
an origin story.

00:14:30.950 --> 00:14:35.750
Thematically, Homecoming builds on everything
that came before in a truly incredible way,

00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:40.430
with direct callbacks to Tony Stark's character
arc that show how different Peter Parker is

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:42.550
from the hero he's trying to impress.

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:48.010
The idea of a superhero universe erupting
around otherwise normal people is the conflict

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:50.029
that drives everything about the movie.

00:14:50.029 --> 00:14:54.000
Take that away and you've still got great
characters, great action, and one of the best

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:55.149
scenes in the franchise.

00:14:55.149 --> 00:14:58.540
With it, though, you've got pure magic.

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:00.290
Thanks for watching!

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:02.790
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:04.890
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

